{"name":"Signal White","hex":"#ecece6","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"signal","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A sequence of states representing an encoded message in a communication channel.","Any variation of a quantity or change in an entity over time that conveys information upon detection."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To indicate; to convey or communicate by a signal.","To communicate with (a person or system) by a signal."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/signal"},{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"}],"entries":[{"title":"Signal","blocks":[{"text":"A signal is both the process and the result of transmission of data over some media accomplished by embedding some variation. Signals are important in multiple subject fields, including signal processing, information theory and biology."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/William_Powell_Frith_The_signal_1858.jpg/330px-William_Powell_Frith_The_signal_1858.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Signal"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Signal_processing_system.png/500px-Signal_processing_system.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Signal_processing_system.png/500px-Signal_processing_system.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Signal transmission using electronic signals","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASignal_processing_system.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Finnish_distant_signal_displaying_Expect_Stop.jpg/120px-Finnish_distant_signal_displaying_Expect_Stop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e5/Finnish_distant_signal_displaying_Expect_Stop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Finnish_distant_signal_displaying_Expect_Stop.jpg/120px-Finnish_distant_signal_displaying_Expect_Stop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFinnish_distant_signal_displaying_Expect_Stop.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Hailing_a_cab.jpg/250px-Hailing_a_cab.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Hailing_a_cab.jpg/330px-Hailing_a_cab.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wik